DP  - 2017
TI  - A Primary Pleural Hydatid Cyst in an Unusual Location.
PG  - 166-169
AB  - Hydatid cyst has a predilection to involve the liver and lungs. Most of the 
      reported cases of intra-pleural hydatid cyst are secondary; primary involvement 
      has rarely been reported in the English-language literature. Here, we report on a 
      33-year-old woman who presented with complaints of dyspnea, cough, low-grade 
      fever, and chills over the previous 3 months. Primary pleural hydatidosis was 
      suspected on abdominopelvic CT; hence, right thoracotomy and cystectomy were 
      performed. Albendazole was administered postoperatively for 6 months. During this 
      period, liver function tests and abdominal sonography results were normal. 
      Despite its rarity, our case emphasizes that general surgeons should suspect 
      primary hydatidosis of the plural cavity when they detect large cystic masses in 
      patients with mediastinal shifting and radiography findings such as white lung, 
      especially in patients with fever and dyspnea.
