TI  - Aerosolized antibiotics for non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis.
PG  - 177-84
LID - 10.1159/000366000 [doi]
AB  - Patients with non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis (NCFB) share many of the 
      respiratory symptoms and the disease progression of cystic fibrosis (CF). As 
      there are no approved therapies for the management of NCFB, an approach has been 
      to use therapies similar to those used to treat CF. In many cases, however, this 
      is ineffective or detrimental. The reason for this has not been determined, but 
      it may be due to key differences in pathogenesis. The questions arising from this 
      have spurred dedicated investigation into the effective management of NCFB. 
      Patients with NCFB have chronic bacterial infection, and bacterial load 
      correlates with symptoms and quality of life. Treatment with systemic antibiotics 
      decreases bacterial load and can have a favorable effect on outcomes. Chronic or 
      frequent use of systemic antibiotics, however, is impractical and sometimes 
      unsafe, so aerosol as a means of delivery is seen as an attractive alternative. 
      The clinical response to and tolerability of inhaled antibiotics have differed 
      significantly between NCFB and CF. New delivery technology, novel antibiotic 
      formulations and a better understanding of the bacterial burden of NCFB are now 
      changing the approach to disease management.
