TI  - Lung transplantation and lung cancer: is there a link?
PG  - 441-5
LID - 10.1159/000326934 [doi]
AB  - Lung transplantation offers a major survival benefit in selected patients with 
      end-stage pulmonary disease. In recent years, there is accumulating evidence of 
      an increase in solid organ tumors and especially in primary lung cancer. There is 
      also some anecdotal evidence that lung transplantation may be beneficial as a 
      treatment option in some patients with lung cancer, such as the ones with 
      extensive bronchoalveolar cell carcinoma. In this review, we analyze the 
      literature data reporting the incidence of lung cancer after lung transplantation 
      with its inherent risk factors, and we summarize the available data on possible 
      treatment of lung cancer by lung transplantation. Finally, bronchial epithelial 
      chimerism as possible cause for lung cancer is discussed.
