VI  - 73
IP  - 4
DP  - 2006
TI  - Intrathoracic sporadic desmoid tumor with the beta-catenin gene mutation in exon 
      3 and activated cyclin D1.
PG  - 558-61
AB  - We report a case of intrathoracic desmoid tumor without familial adenomatous 
      polyposis and demonstrate beta-catenin mutation of exon 3. A 15-year-old male 
      presented with a desmoid tumor after having sustained an assault. In an 
      examination for a mutation of the beta-catenin gene, an activating mutation from 
      ACC (Thr) to GCC (Ala) at codon 41 was found. Immunohistochemical staining showed 
      that accumulated beta-catenin protein was predominantly localized in the nuclei 
      of desmoid cells, and cyclin D1 protein was also overexpressed. These findings 
      might suggest that an activating mutation of the beta-catenin gene affected 
      regulation of the cyclin D1 gene, resulting in the generation of intrathoracic 
      sporadic desmoid tumor, which arose at the site of posttraumatic injury.
