VI  - 69
IP  - 6
DP  - 2002
TI  - Abnormalities on cardiopulmonary exercise test in a dyspneic patient. A case 
      report of unsuspected pulmonary embolism.
PG  - 543-6
AB  - The cardiopulmonary exercise test (CPET) is frequently used for the quantitative 
      evaluation of exercise tolerance and for the qualitative assessment of the origin 
      of dyspnea. Although reasonably safe, CPET is contraindicated in acute clinical 
      situations and relatively contraindicated in severe pulmonary hypertension. 
      Pulmonary emboli are usually present during the acute onset of pulmonary 
      hypertension; however, in situations where pulmonary vascular disease is more 
      unsuspected, CPET may help with the diagnosis. We present a report of a 
      47-year-old Caucasian female with a 2-month history of exertional dyspnea. She 
      had a normal pulmonary function test, arterial blood gas analysis and CT scan of 
      the chest. On CPET she was found to have a higher than normal physiological dead 
      space to tidal volume ratio, increased ventilatory equivalent for the elimination 
      of CO(2) (V(E)/VCO(2)), persistently positive arterial-end-tidal PCO(2 
      )difference at peak exercise, and a low anaerobic threshold suggestive of 
      pulmonary vascular disease. She was subsequently diagnosed with pulmonary embolic 
      disease by her ventilation/perfusion scan and pulmonary arteriogram. This report 
      highlights the importance of recognizing the hallmarks of unsuspected pulmonary 
      vascular disease on CPET.
