TI  - Mechanisms of pleural involvement in orphan diseases.
PG  - 5-12
LID - 10.1159/000335128 [doi]
AB  - Over the past 10 years, the widespread clinical applicability of semi-invasive 
      and noninvasive diagnostic tools including medical thoracoscopy and 
      ultrasonography has expanded the occurrence of pleural effusions to include 
      several rare diseases such as granulomatous, connective tissue and autoimmune 
      disorders including sarcoidosis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's), 
      systemic sclerosis, lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, Sjögren's 
      syndrome, amyloidosis, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, and others. The purpose of 
      this review is to summarize the current state of the knowledge regarding 
      pathogenetic mechanisms of pleural involvement in rare disease entities and to 
      highlight the need for more efforts to understand the underlying mechanisms for a 
      more effective therapy.
