DP  - 2007
TI  - Pulmonary infection with Cryptococcus neoformans in the face of underlying 
      sarcoidosis.
PG  - 462-6
AB  - We present a case of limited pulmonary cryptococcal infection following exposure 
      to pigeon excreta in a patient with sarcoidosis. The pathogenicity of 
      Cryptococcus neoformans depends on the interplay between the immune status of the 
      host and the virulence of the infecting strain. It can range from asymptomatic 
      lung colonization in the immunocompetent host to rapidly progressive meningitis 
      in immunocompromised patients. Immunological models of respiratory disease 
      emphasize a distinction between infections associated with immune suppression on 
      the one hand and diseases such as sarcoidosis believed to involve an excessive 
      Th1-mediated immune response on the other. This case exemplifies the complex 
      nature of immunological responses in the lung and highlights the importance of 
      considering the possibility of co-existent fungal infection in individuals with 
      sarcoidosis. Novel immunotherapeutic options for cryptococcal infection are 
      discussed.
