TI  - Cavernous hemangiomas of lungs and liver in an asymptomatic girl.
PG  - 647-50
AB  - We describe a 15-year-old asymptomatic girl with multiple pulmonary nodules which 
      turned out to be cavernous hemangiomas. The radiological findings of other organs 
      revealed no abnormalities, excluding the liver, where multiple low-density areas 
      were located. Thoracoscopy and laparoscopy revealed cavernous hemangiomas of the 
      lung and liver. While liver cavernous hemangiomas are common, pulmonary 
      hemangiomas are fairly rare. Although cavernous hemangiomas are classified as 
      malformations, some of these lesions have been reported to grow. In pulmonary 
      hemangiomas, some cases have an inexorable clinical course with hemoptysis, 
      respiratory distress, and heart failure. In our case, the lesions have not 
      remarkably progressed under observation for more than 2 years.
