TI  - Mycobacterium avium complex pleuritis.
PG  - 547-9
AB  - Non-tuberculous mycobacterium infection is rarely accompanied by pleural 
      involvement. We report a very rare case of Mycobacterium avium-intracellurare 
      complex (MAC) pleuritis with massive pleural effusion. The patient was a 
      non-compromised 67-year-old female and had been treated for pulmonary 
      non-tuberculous mycobacterium infection. She was admitted to hospital because of 
      general malaise, low-grade fever and right pleural effusion. Cytological 
      examination of the effusion did not show malignant cells. MAC was only identified 
      by culture and PCR. No other bacteria were detected. Complete resolution of the 
      pleural effusion occurred after administration of anti-tubercular agents 
      (isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol) and clarithromycin.
