TI  - Effect of body position on arterial oxygen tension in the elderly.
PG  - 123-8
AB  - BACKGROUND: It is well known that body position can have an effect on gas 
      exchange though the magnitude of this effect has not been studied thoroughly in 
      the elderly. OBJECTIVES: This study analyzes the effect body position change has 
      on arterial oxygen tension (PaO(2)) and arterial carbon dioxide tension (PaCO(2)) 
      in healthy elderly. METHODS: We tested 46 "lung-healthy" elderly, including 30 
      women and 16 men, 67-88 years of age. Blood was drawn from the radial artery 
      first in the sitting position and subsequently in the supine position. Spirometry 
      was performed. RESULTS: Mean (SD) sitting PaO(2) was 10.53 kPa (1.22), whereas 
      mean supine PaO(2) was 9.85 kPa (1.33). The difference between sitting and supine 
      PaO(2) was 0.68 kPa (0.86) and was statistically significant. Sitting PaCO(2) was 
      5.06 kPa (0.47) and supine PaCO(2) was 5.05 kPa (0.54). The difference between 
      sitting and supine PaO(2) correlated positively with FEV(1)/FVC %, negatively 
      with the corresponding difference between sitting and supine PaCO(2), and 
      negatively with BMI. CONCLUSIONS: We conclude that the significant difference in 
      PaO(2) in sitting and supine positions clearly shows that the position needs to 
      be considered both when attempting to establish reference values and when 
      evaluating gas exchange in elderly persons. The positional changes in oxygenation 
      are related to the corresponding change in PaCO(2), and to FEV(1)/FVC % and BMI.
