TI  - Thoracoscopic electrode implantation for diaphragm pacing in dogs.
PG  - 69-74
AB  - BACKGROUND: Diaphragm pacing is an attractive method of ventilatory support; 
      however, it requires electrode implantation to the phrenic nerve or diaphragm. 
      The thoracic approach is favored for several reasons, and it usually accompanies 
      invasive bilateral thoracotomy. OBJECTIVES: This study was conducted to develop a 
      new electrode suitable for video-assisted thoracoscopic implantation, which is 
      less invasive than the conventional thoracic approach. METHODS: The feasibility 
      of video-assisted thoracoscopic implantation was tested with newly designed 
      electrodes using 5 mongrel dogs. Furthermore, diaphragm pacing was performed for 
      60 min to test whether or not the implanted electrodes were functional. RESULTS: 
      Video-assisted electrode implantation was successful in all 5 cases. No 
      complications occurred during the implantation procedure. In acute-phase pacing 
      trials, the electrodes stimulated the phrenic nerves for 60 min without any 
      pacing failures. The mean value of PaCO(2) increased gradually from 32.2 +/- 
      (SEM) 1.52 to 54.6 +/- 4.58 mm Hg, and the value of tidal volume decreased 
      gradually from 242.9 +/- 31.3 to 147.5 +/- 24.5 ml in 60 min pacing. CONCLUSIONS: 
      The thoracoscopic implantation of new electrodes was less invasive, and was a 
      safe procedure for diaphragm pacing. Meticulous care should be taken to avoid 
      muscle fatigue.
