TI  - Is there a need for another inhalative beta(2)-agonist besides formoterol in 
      patients with asthma?
PG  - 432-7
AB  - Formoterol can substitute the rapid- and short-acting beta(2)-agonists as well as 
      the slow- and long-acting salmeterol. Therefore formoterol in a fixed combination 
      with an inhalant steroid reduces the aerosol devices necessary for asthma control 
      to only one, to be used for regular "controller" and, as needed, "rescue 
      therapy". The side effect profile of formoterol is comparable to the short-acting 
      beta(2)-agonists which makes the combination with a topically active 
      glucocorticoid applicable in patients of any asthma severity as long as they are 
      able to perform an inspiratory vital capacity maneuver
