TI  - Real-world long-term efficacy of chemoimmunotherapy in advanced stage small cell 
      lung cancer.
PG  - 1-26
LID - 10.1159/000553352 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Since IMPOWER-133 and CASPIAN phase 3 trials, chemoimmunotherapy 
      has become the first-line systemic treatment for advanced stage small-cell lung 
      carcinoma (SCLC). We conducted a study on the long-term efficacy. METHODS: A 
      retrospective cohort study was performed on advanced stage SCLC patients who 
      initiated treatment with first-line carboplatin-etoposide-atezolizumab between 
      January 2018 and December 2022 at the University Hospitals Leuven. Follow-up data 
      were collected until February 2025. The Kaplan Meier method was used for 
      estimating survival outcomes, and Cox regression models were used to assess the 
      association between baseline clinical characteristics (age, ECOG Performance 
      Status, metastatic sites, s-albumin, s-LDH, s-CRP, s-NSE, Neutrophil-Lymphocyte 
      Ratio (NLR), modified Glasgow Prognostic Score (mGPS) and LIPI score) and 
      survival outcomes. RESULTS: A total of 74 patients were included. The median 
      progression-free survival (PFS) was 4.8 months (95%CI 4.5-5.0) and median overall 
      survival (OS) was 11.5 months (95%CI 8.9-14.6). The 1-year, 2-year and 5-year PFS 
      and OS rates were 16.2% (95%CI 8.9-25.5), 9.5% (95%CI 4.2-17.4) and 8.1% (95%CI 
      3.3-15.7), and 47.3% (95%CI 35.6-58.1), 18.9% (95%CI 11.0-28.5) and 8.9% (95%CI 
      3.2-18.2%), respectively. In multivariable analysis, bone metastasis (HR 2.21; 
      95%CI 1.26-3.87), age (+1yr, HR 1.03; 95%CI 1.00-1.07), s-NSE >250 µg/L (HR 4.55; 
      95%CI 2.13-9.72), and s-albumin ≥35g/dl (HR 0.49; 95%CI 0.25-0.99) were 
      significantly associated with OS. During induction phase, chemotherapy dose 
      reduction and discontinuation occurred in 31% and 8%, respectively. Overall, 
      irAEs occurred in 21 patients (28%) leading to discontinuation of atezolizumab in 
      7 patients (10%). CONCLUSION: Our study provides long-term safety and efficacy 
      outcomes of first-line chemoimmunotherapy in a real-world cohort of advanced 
      stage SCLC. In multivariable analysis, we found that baseline conventional 
      biomarkers (age, bone metastasis, s-albumin, and s-NSE) did significantly predict 
      overall survival, while systemic inflammation scores (NLR, mGPS and LIPI) did 
      not.
CI  - S. Karger AG, Basel.
