TI  - Venobronchial fistula: an unusual complication of long-term central venous 
      access.
PG  - 686-9
AB  - A venobronchial fistula developed between the azygous vein and the upper aspect 
      of the right main bronchus 12 months after completion of the treatment of a stage 
      IIIB non-small-cell lung cancer in a 54-year-old man. The fistula contained the 
      tip of the catheter placed for chemotherapy perfusion. The reported case 
      presented risk factors previously identified for such a complication. In 
      addition, some clinical particularities were present, suggesting new potent risk 
      factors and some preventive means for safe long-term central venous 
      catheterization.
