TI  - Duro-pleural fistula diagnosed by beta2-transferrin.
PG  - 423-5
AB  - An 81-year-old man was referred for evaluation of a chronic transudative pleural 
      effusion that required 8 therapeutic thoracenteses over 11 months for relief of 
      dyspnea. Extensive lumbar disk surgery had been performed 2 years prior to his 
      onset of dyspnea. The diagnosis of duro-pleural fistula was confirmed by finding 
      the presence of beta2-transferrin in the pleural fluid. The 'water-like' pleural 
      fluid had a total protein of <1 gm/dl, an LDH of 92 IU/l, a glucose of 101 mg/dl, 
      and pH of 7.55. beta2-Transferrin has a sensitivity approaching 100% and a 
      specificity of 95% in identifying CSF leaks from head trauma. To our knowledge, 
      this is the first report of beta2-transferrin in pleural fluid from a 
      duro-pleural fistula.
