TI  - Pulmonary emboli caused by iliac compression syndrome without leg symptoms.
PG  - 269-72
AB  - Iliac compression syndrome is a clinical condition that occurs as a result of 
      compression of the left common iliac vein by the overlying right common iliac 
      artery. This syndrome most often affects young to middle-aged women, and patients 
      usually have left leg symptoms. We report the unusual case of an 18-year-old male 
      who had pulmonary emboli caused by iliac compression syndrome without leg 
      symptoms. Combined venography and aortography confirmed the diagnosis. The 
      patient was successfully treated with anticoagulants and vena cava filter 
      insertion. Iliac compression syndrome should be considered when pulmonary 
      embolism appears without obvious cause.
