TI  - Addition of ATP and MgCl2 to the preservation solution attenuates lung 
      reperfusion injury following cold ischemia.
PG  - 292-8
AB  - BACKGROUND: In lung transplantation, reperfusion injury following cold ischemia 
      is one of the crucial problems for recipients. OBJECTIVE: We evaluated the 
      protective effect of adding a combination of ATP and MgCl2 to the preservation 
      solution against lung reperfusion injury following cold ischemia. METHODS: Using 
      an isolated rat lung perfusion model with fresh rat blood as the perfusate, the 
      rats were divided into five groups (n = 6). In the fresh group, the study lungs 
      were flushed with phosphate-buffered saline (PBS), then immediately reperfused 
      for 120 min. In the control group, the study lungs were flushed with PBS, then 
      cold ischemia was induced for 9 h (4 degrees C), after which reperfusion was 
      performed. In the other three groups, the protocols were the same as for the 
      control group except that ATP and/or MgCl2 were added to the PBS: ATP group (100 
      microM ATP), MgCl2 group (100 microM MgCl2) and ATP + MgCl2 group (100 microM ATP 
      + 100 microM MgCl2). RESULTS: In the ATP + MgCl2 group, the intrapulmonary shunt 
      fraction, peak airway pressure and wet to dry lung weight ratio were 
      significantly lower than those in the control group. No improvement was observed 
      in the ATP or MgCl2 groups. Histological examination supported these 
      physiological results. In all groups, flush time and lipid peroxide levels in the 
      lungs after cold ischemia did not show any significant differences. CONCLUSION: 
      The addition of ATP and MgCl2 to the preservation solution attenuated reperfusion 
      injury following cold ischemia in rat lungs.
