TI  - Pathogenesis of lung disease in cystic fibrosis.
PG  - 3-8
AB  - Lung disease in cystic fibrosis is primarily due to a defect in the cystic 
      fibrosis transmembrane regulating protein (CFTR). This results in abnormal 
      chloride transfer across epithelial membranes causing an excessively viscid mucus 
      lining of the airways. Bacterial invasion particularly with Staphylococcus 
      aureus, Haemophilus influenzae and Pseudomonas aeruginosa stimulates a vigorous 
      and excessive primarily neutrophil-driven inflammatory response throughout the 
      lungs. Products of this inflammation not only damage incoming bacteria but also 
      the host tissue itself. Over a period of years this chronic suppurative process 
      results in permanent ongoing lung destruction principally manifested as bilateral 
      bronchiectasis.
