TI  - Age- and Sex-Stratified Population-Based Estimates of Hospitalization-Requiring 
      Asthma Exacerbation in Korea during Prepandemic Period (2002-2019).
PG  - 1-10
LID - 10.1159/000551953 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Asthma exacerbations vary in prevalence, risk factors, and outcomes 
      according to sex and age. However, large-scale longitudinal studies stratifying 
      the prevalence of asthma exacerbation by age and sex are limited. METHODS: This 
      observational national sample cohort study used data from the National Health 
      Insurance database to assess the prevalence of hospitalization requiring asthma 
      exacerbation in Korea in 2002-2019. The study population included 1,137,861 
      participants, categorized by sex and age. The participants were classified into 
      six age groups: <2 years (infants and toddlers), 2-5 years (preschoolers), 6-12 
      years (children), 12-18 years (adolescents), 19-59 years (adults), and ≥60 years 
      (older adults). Asthma exacerbation was defined as hospitalization with a primary 
      diagnosis of asthma or status asthmaticus on an annual basis. Trends in annual 
      prevalence were analyzed using joinpoint regression, and the annual average 
      percentage change (AAPC) for each segment was calculated. RESULTS: Over the 
      entire age range, the prevalence of asthma exacerbation increased from 5.65 cases 
      per 10,000 person-years (PY) in 2002 to 11.89 in 2010 and decreased to 5.73 in 
      2019. This trend was consistent in male and female individuals. Infants and 
      toddlers, preschoolers, and older adults exhibited the highest prevalence, 
      peaking at 87.14, 84.47, and 22.96 cases per 10,000 PY in 2006-2007, 
      respectively, and subsequently declining. In contrast, the peak incidences in 
      adolescents and adults were 4.76 and 3.26 cases per 10,000 PY, respectively, 
      which were delayed until 2013-2014. During the study period, the AAPC decreased 
      in infants and toddlers as well as preschoolers but increased in age groups after 
      school age, with a dominant increase of 10.7 (from 7.02 to 17.1) noted in 
      adolescents. Mortality due to asthma exacerbation increased significantly among 
      the older population. Among individuals aged under 12 years, asthma exacerbations 
      were more prevalent in male individuals; however, this trend was reversed among 
      adults and older adults, with higher rates observed in female individuals. 
      CONCLUSION: This nationwide longitudinal study highlights trends in asthma 
      exacerbation prevalence that vary by sex and age during an 18-year period, 
      indicating the need to tailor asthma exacerbation prevention strategies in 
      response to these changes.
