DP  - 2014
TI  - Targeting inhaled therapy beyond the lungs.
PG  - 353-4
AB  - Pulmonary disease has been the primary target of inhaled therapeutics for over 50 
      years. During that period, increasing interest has arisen in the use of this 
      route of administration to gain access to the systemic circulation for the 
      treatment of a number of diseases beyond the airways. In order to effectively 
      employ this route, the barriers to transport from the lungs following deposition 
      of aerosols must be considered, including the nature of the disease (whether 
      proximal, as in pulmonary hypertension, or distal, as in diabetes). Delivery to 
      the systemic circulation begins with the efficiency of aerosol generation and 
      subsequent deposition in the airways and proceeds to the influence of mechanisms 
      of clearance, including absorption, metabolism, and mucociliary and cell-mediated 
      transport, on the residence time of the drugs in the lungs. The nature of the 
      drug (small or large molecules/low or high molecular weight), susceptibility to 
      degradation and general physicochemical properties play a role in the chemistry 
      of its formulation, physics of aerosol delivery and biology of disposition.
