TI  - Pleural effusions in critically ill patients.
PG  - 241-8
LID - 10.1159/000143108 [doi]
AB  - Pleural effusions (PEs) are common in critically ill patients mainly as a 
      consequence of severe cardiopulmonary disorders frequently encountered in these 
      patients. Their impact on the pathophysiology of acute respiratory failure 
      remains unknown. They are usually small and uncomplicated transudates that are 
      easily overlooked on a supine portable chest X-ray and do not require drainage or 
      infectious exudates that always require thoracocentesis. The diagnosis of PEs in 
      critically ill patients has been revolutionized with the advent of chest 
      ultrasound allowing easy bedside quantification of pleural fluid and making 
      thoracocentesis a safe procedure especially in high-risk patients on mechanical 
      ventilation. CT provides a much more accurate evaluation of the size and location 
      of PEs and is extremely helpful in the guidance of catheters into loculated fluid 
      collections. Hemothorax in critically ill patients is usually related to trauma 
      or surgical interventions and requires early drainage and possibly surgical 
      exploration.
