DP  - 2009
TI  - Acute eosinophilic pneumonia complicating Coccidioides immitis pneumonia: a case 
      report and literature review.
PG  - 102-6
AB  - Though peripheral eosinophilia commonly accompanies infection with Coccidioides 
      immitis, pulmonary eosinophilia in patients with C. immitis and multilobar 
      infiltrates has been reported in only three prior cases to our knowledge. To 
      enhance clinicians' suspicion of an unusual complication of C. immitis pneumonia 
      and the treatment implications, we present a patient with an initial diagnosis of 
      acute eosinophilic pneumonia [based on bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid 
      analysis and transbronchial biopsy] followed several days later by a diagnosis of 
      accompanying C. immitis pneumonia when BAL fluid cultures grew fungus. In keeping 
      with the suspicion of acute eosinophilic pneumonia, the patient responded 
      dramatically to corticosteroids before antifungal therapy was initiated. This 
      patient with pulmonary eosinophilia complicating C. immitis pneumonia extends 
      earlier reports of a hypersensitivity-like reaction to C. immitis pneumonia and, 
      based on the dramatic acute clinical response, reminds clinicians of the 
      potential adjunctive role of corticosteroids. At the same time, the possible 
      adverse effects of using corticosteroids in patients with fungal disease warrants 
      caution and close follow-up in patients for whom steroids are prescribed.
