TI  - Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis: evaluation with positron emission tomography.
PG  - 197-202
AB  - BACKGROUND: The pathogenesis of interstitial lung disease remains under 
      investigation, but may be related to increased inflammatory or cellular activity. 
      This activity may be detectable with physiologic imaging. OBJECTIVES: We 
      investigated the role of physiologic imaging using 
      (18)F-2-fluoro-2-deoxy-D-glucose ((18)FDG)-positron emission tomography (PET) 
      scans in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF). METHODS: Seven male patients with 
      histologically confirmed IPF underwent (18)FDG-PET scans. Scans were analyzed 
      qualitatively and interpreted as positive or negative. Patients also underwent 
      pulmonary function tests and computed tomography (CT) scans. RESULTS: The average 
      total lung capacity was 71 +/- 22% predicted (mean +/- SD) and diffusing capacity 
      for carbon monoxide was 44 +/- 14% predicted. All had changes consistent with IPF 
      on chest CT and 2 patients had ground glass attenuation. Six of seven patients 
      (86%) had a positive (18)FDG-PET scan. Changes in the (18)FDG-PET scan were seen 
      in 1 patient corresponding to changes in clinical status. CONCLUSIONS: Our 
      findings suggest that (18)FDG-PET scans may be helpful in the evaluation of IPF. 
      Increased activity suggests active disease and changes in response to therapy.
