TI  - An adult case of Bochdalek hernia complicated with hemothorax.
PG  - 644-6
AB  - A 53-year-old female with mild shock due to vomiting and abdominal pain visited 
      the emergency room of our hospital. Chest X-ray on admission showed a large 
      amount of left pleural effusion. Thoracentesis revealed hemorrhagic pleural 
      effusion. An upper gastrointestinal series showed interruption of the upper 
      gastric body, but the anal side was not visualized. Contrast X-ray examination of 
      the thoracic cavity via the drainage tube demonstrated intrathoracic herniation 
      through the diaphragm. These findings suggested gastric impaction in the foramen 
      of Bochdalek, and thoracotomy was immediately performed. A black-colored stomach 
      and greater omentum, suggesting necrotic changes, were observed in the thoracic 
      cavity, and there was bleeding from the greater omentum. Resection of the 
      necrotic organs and closure of the hernial orifice were followed by good 
      recovery.
