TI  - Quantification of the size of primary spontaneous pneumothorax: accuracy of the 
      Light index.
PG  - 396-9
AB  - BACKGROUND: The size of a pneumothorax (PTX) is usually estimated by the Light 
      index. Treatment strategies of (primary, spontaneous) PTX partially depend upon 
      the size of the PTX. To our knowledge, the Light index has not yet been 
      correlated with the actual volume of the PTX. OBJECTIVES: To correlate the 
      estimated size of a primary spontaneous PTX by means of the Light index, with the 
      actual amount of air present in the pleural space. METHODS: Actual PTX volumes 
      were measured by means of manual aspiration of air present in the pleural space 
      in 18 patients with primary spontaneous PTX and correlated with the size 
      estimation obtained by the Light index. RESULTS: Light index and volume 
      measurements were strongly correlated (r = 0.84, p < 0.0001). CONCLUSIONS: The 
      Light index is a good estimate of the actual size of a (primary spontaneous) PTX.
